The most common modern approach is Flexbox or Grid.

## Flexbox

```css
.parent {
  display: flex;
  justify-content: center; /* horizontal */
  align-items: center;     /* vertical */
  min-height: 100vh;       /* parent needs a height */
}
```

## Grid (shortest)

```css
.parent {
  display: grid;
  place-items: center;
  min-height: 100vh;
}
```

## Absolute positioning (when you need to take the element out of flow)

```css
.parent {
  position: relative;
  height: 100vh;
}

.child {
  position: absolute;
  top: 50%;
  left: 50%;
  transform: translate(-50%, -50%);
}
```

## Margin auto with Flexbox

```css
.parent {
  display: flex;
  min-height: 100vh;
}

.child {
  margin: auto;
}
```

## Notes

- The parent must have a defined height, otherwise there's no extra space to center within. `100vh` centers in the viewport; use a fixed height or `100%` (with a parent chain that has height) for other cases.
- To center just one axis, use only `justify-content` (horizontal) or `align-items` (vertical) with Flexbox.
- For horizontally centering a block element with a set width, `margin: 0 auto;` still works.

**Recommendation:** use `display: grid; place-items: center;` for simplicity, or Flexbox if you're already using it for layout.

The most common modern approach is flexbox or grid on the parent.

## Flexbox

```css
.parent {
  display: flex;
  justify-content: center; /* horizontal */
  align-items: center;     /* vertical */
  min-height: 100vh;       /* parent needs a height */
}
```

## Grid (shortest)

```css
.parent {
  display: grid;
  place-items: center;
  min-height: 100vh;
}
```

## Absolute positioning (when you can't change the parent's display)

```css
.parent {
  position: relative;
  height: 100vh;
}

.child {
  position: absolute;
  top: 50%;
  left: 50%;
  transform: translate(-50%, -50%);
}
```

## Margin auto (flex child)

```css
.parent {
  display: flex;
  height: 100vh;
}

.child {
  margin: auto;
}
```

## Notes

- The parent must have a defined height, otherwise there is no extra vertical space to center within. `100vh` fills the viewport; use `100dvh` on mobile to account for dynamic browser toolbars.
- To center within the whole page, apply the styles to `body` (or a wrapper) and remove default margins if you see scrollbars.
- `place-items: center` is shorthand for `align-items: center` and `justify-items: center`.

For most cases, I'd use the grid version: it's two lines and easy to remember.

The simplest modern approach is **CSS Grid**:

```css
.parent {
  display: grid;
  place-items: center;
  min-height: 100vh; /* parent needs a height */
}
```

```html
<div class="parent">
  <div class="child">Centered</div>
</div>
```

**Flexbox** works just as well:

```css
.parent {
  display: flex;
  justify-content: center; /* horizontal */
  align-items: center;     /* vertical */
  min-height: 100vh;
}
```

**Absolute positioning** (useful for overlays and modals):

```css
.parent {
  position: relative;
}

.child {
  position: absolute;
  top: 50%;
  left: 50%;
  transform: translate(-50%, -50%);
}
```

Or without a transform, if the child has a known or fit-content size:

```css
.child {
  position: absolute;
  inset: 0;
  margin: auto;
  width: fit-content;
  height: fit-content;
}
```

**Notes**
- The parent must have a height for vertical centering to be visible. `100vh`, `100dvh` (better on mobile), or any fixed height works.
- For horizontal-only centering of a block element, `margin-inline: auto` with a set `width` is enough.
- Grid's `place-items: center` is shorthand for `align-items` and `justify-items`. Use it when you want the shortest code.

Use grid on the parent:

```css
.parent {
  display: grid;
  place-items: center;
  min-height: 100vh; /* parent needs a height */
}
```

⚠ Centering fails silently if the parent has no explicit height, so set one.

Put this on the parent so it centers the child on both axes:

```css
.parent {
  display: grid;
  place-items: center;
  min-height: 100vh; /* parent needs a height */
}
```

⚠ Centering fails silently if the parent has no explicit height, so set one (e.g. `min-height: 100vh`).

